gned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530195} :answer-field/id #uuid "21a921da-7c82-54fc-aa04-c89d5a085fc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530243}] :question/id #uuid "ccdd18fc-f395-5a18-a92a-23c7269d12d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80912" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e08605f-fa3a-569b-af1b-d45a70992915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556772} {:answer/id #uuid "d5cefb37-7da0-53cb-8ec1-47dc88f4f3e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556950} {:answer/id #uuid "da268bf5-cb3d-56a1-95c2-03e31ddbd921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556971} {:answer/id #uuid "f787f3d0-ca6e-5982-8896-c3df5a7de145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557043} {:answer/id #uuid "fd03625b-a11f-58bd-a340-4e7487dfc2c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556950} :answer-field/id #uuid "45a64cfe-8191-5b79-aaff-a5627b1f6695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557104}] :question/id #uuid "cb79f743-bef1-5e9c-b5b3-1f6ceebb3420" :question/math-academy-id "q-80911" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157074 :question/id #uuid "f1eaa2b8-544c-5217-82a5-8ec0a311c5a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7049" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n![](images/d6/d66051007ab78d2727f3b501156878a369a04b24bca337ed587feb07935a1de7.svg)\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n![](images/bf/bfd809b436c24efcb2e9934b1c36293dfecb4bf87ab41c05f8cbbb9d506ea34d.svg)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/7e/7e191da3b082da420622418962e7491a6e78b6ed8192259b9b50ec8040bbc525.svg)\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda=-4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/fb/fb833263882adc2422083c111e96b0e030263f93e4c0174082f0f57667b46e8e.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/fe/fe33e849a0e3baa7e8547c9559f9c36e980b8e715cba6e26900eea5d815e9b30.svg)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-4+4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4+4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/2c/2c443847a5559737066873593b399117c03e9c8141b403bc7f820c9ad493e2a9.svg)\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_2 = 1.\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_1$ is a free variable. Setting $w_1 = 0,$ we get \n![](images/00/00b635d3858d38c41ad9e1bacb00ed4c6370b5e2112aa82faa6ef37a83209a6c.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/d1/d1911a797bfa5915fb1478c91dc007ca3f1419b1093180dfb08932a008337b6e.svg)\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0, b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e2f4047-28db-5982-bf27-62bd47c9eb17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300185} {:answer/id #uuid "42e4a570-7f1b-547f-b489-c83eaeafeeac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300290} {:answer/id #uuid "4b99766b-da02-5b51-a022-313d44a16524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300354} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8e3287-39f8-532f-b6b0-a87ef89feb4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300595} {:answer/id #uuid "e27d740d-be6d-5d25-b6a0-e68ad85f227e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300595} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e08cf9-f383-515d-a856-21fa02542b1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301863}] :question/id #uuid "f38771d6-22f6-588d-95e5-d42dd40123e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-325120" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2-2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97d27613-14ae-5fb9-85e6-7f2fedaa1566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155267} {:answer/id #uuid "efad71c3-10ab-5a59-bf6c-a42ebfbdc788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155268} {:answer/id #uuid "49c32918-f77c-5a03-bbc7-926b395b95e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155269} {:answer/id #uuid "71e8d9ff-563e-5f8b-abf5-4f25947b5c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155270} {:answer/id #uuid "73ea148b-506d-59ad-ae3b-1953b71205bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155271}] :answer-field/id #uuid "41ba843b-06e7-542e-932f-ea2517cd8f8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155266}] :question/id #uuid "353f3d53-45fc-5221-8de9-cda630722722" :question/math-academy-id "q-325134" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$.\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n$x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + c_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}te^{3t} + \\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t}), c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b$?\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $λ = 3$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22383ef7-ab1c-515a-a72e-c4e9aa9f6988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345659} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc156ee-b890-56ae-9265-698a328be38e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345739} {:answer/id #uuid "621ed4b6-5f67-5f28-8aae-baafd82b14df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346208} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c6f31e-ee9f-5550-9d88-f355ac4329ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346934} {:answer/id #uuid "edabc339-22bc-5e3d-8b82-b8a9bd907662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345659} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46662e4-7532-5853-895f-d3089153ed44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348003}] :question/id #uuid "08ad4637-1a63-55fa-b32f-7dbcbe48ebf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-80603" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-2.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1+2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3+2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}-{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{1}-{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=1,$ and $a+b=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f8430ae-f2df-5901-af99-08b9afe18d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346325} {:answer/id #uuid "955c6786-041f-56d1-a8a2-ce05b9f436cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346654} {:answer/id #uuid "98ba621e-111e-5691-b61d-7fcf4899d462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346683} {:answer/id #uuid "c75a9cd4-47b9-5ee1-a928-ce3a55928be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347084} {:answer/id #uuid "ea629320-0d6b-58a0-800f-b0f90fa23465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347084} :answer-field/id #uuid "f114ab66-f9f7-54e4-9082-d4ad0a1947a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348028}] :question/id #uuid "fa98e78d-c213-5ff9-b494-b8ef24528f71" :question/math-academy-id "q-80604" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1+1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3+1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=-2,b=-1,$ and $a+b=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2619f6f2-5cc8-5f8c-8f16-944b7395e987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348775} {:answer/id #uuid "5a778041-0918-5cad-b3f3-76a35dc3cd5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349280} {:answer/id #uuid "6e91384e-efa6-5dac-9793-7e567ae41ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349460} {:answer/id #uuid "e0962c91-2829-51a7-97be-2fbe699aa460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350420} {:answer/id #uuid "f167108e-4cb2-5fec-8ae9-0c9fde6d55c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350556} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a9d7e30-8cf8-5daf-935e-58ce2c4e1e40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350755}] :question/id #uuid "cae2c390-9a2f-587b-be5b-7ff082d424d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80915" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3+3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3+3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+5{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f60048-7d7c-5687-9ef2-9d7a40e3cd6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348782} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce3934e-26ad-50c3-b8ad-8eb9aeefecd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348840} {:answer/id #uuid "34571999-a224-5304-b6f7-661d75ad824f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348917} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5c0860-4e19-5581-8cad-9596b9de6b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349840} {:answer/id #uuid "c2004d97-ccfe-5f1f-a183-c402f97fd670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349840} :answer-field/id #uuid "35dc67a9-2544-5970-b757-6ab8a5b07cd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350799}] :question/id #uuid "ef21bab9-f763-5bd8-b177-dccd5650b9c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80918" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =5.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}7-5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=\\frac{3}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1570ae2-7522-574c-b09e-cc0720cebf4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398715} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4fc918-4e9b-5f09-b20e-cf2be22cf7ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398716} {:answer/id #uuid "ab281b2e-5a53-5672-8da4-e676a03d7e36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398717} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0ec4cd-48eb-5345-9138-6da28aeb3e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398718} {:answer/id #uuid "56280ebc-3607-5e92-83e5-9a26a408be13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398718} :answer-field/id #uuid "12200322-ef19-5f83-936e-a28151c6b492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398714}] :question/id #uuid "17c5a462-c29c-5b82-b891-ee8c95c1220f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326236" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1-(-1) & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1-(-1)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66613f28-b688-5af5-87c4-a12f46f9bd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399140} {:answer/id #uuid "b467a51e-f9be-5bed-89bc-7ff012c894de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399141} {:answer/id #uuid "149698b5-3361-5f52-a32c-6ed656acfe8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399142} {:answer/id #uuid "748a5bc0-46b6-5698-846f-66d6952f985d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399143} {:answer/id #uuid "99a4d6e3-caf9-544f-ba67-8f950954be82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399140} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2967834-56c7-54af-a045-1eb688f37565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399139}] :question/id #uuid "8179a1bc-9dd2-5c9c-a7dd-3de3a7cd250c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80917" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+2{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eba8c50d-8cd6-567a-b472-75707ce03343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399147} {:answer/id #uuid "5e646bbf-5ae7-5023-a552-e22b215170d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399148} {:answer/id #uuid "6f01c0dd-442a-5b90-8b62-5974e56662f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399149} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d6bed6-49f3-574f-a78d-c14c6e9719ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399150} {:answer/id #uuid "645e0e17-2268-567a-ac17-75ba1595de6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399148} :answer-field/id #uuid "162ccab1-6783-5e36-870b-96a5c241f52b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399146}] :question/id #uuid "1bd476b7-be13-5d1b-9c4d-aad2517551db" :question/math-academy-id "q-80919" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1+1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1+1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dfffefc-7dc4-503a-9b1f-a84d08149c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413826} {:answer/id #uuid "672f41ac-64c5-58b5-868f-7c1ad340ab36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413827} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa05af9-3949-5d3f-84eb-7ccc92942416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413828} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7ac799-30a1-5efa-892d-6a6142f8760c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413829} {:answer/id #uuid "fb961936-d8ed-5956-ad00-aabdf66df6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413829} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eaed666-f3ff-5e76-bdcf-86e2bff8289c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413831}] :question/id #uuid "9318e8aa-b34c-5ef4-bb58-533fb20cecad" :question/math-academy-id "q-80606" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2-1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4-1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{w}_{1}+9{w}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+3{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=3{w}_{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=3,b=-1,$ and $a+b=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6123a8e7-8566-539c-a378-251b6266a004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529818} {:answer/id #uuid "afc1305e-c6de-5db9-8835-27482372478c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530019} {:answer/id #uuid "dd60fa24-2113-573f-927a-cc5cc09eff1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530126} {:answer/id #uuid "f239d215-8bad-5494-ad9f-a7e4dc4827bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530182} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb02d5f-3761-5753-87fa-efd0f4de2d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530214} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ffbd89f-d981-524e-9095-fdb0dc1768f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530327}] :question/id #uuid "c42bb2d4-33f1-5601-a069-ea5fef63539f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326237" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4-4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157447 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157075 :question/id #uuid "38f7a261-b58c-5ce1-ab77-6bfb5ce40b19" :question/math-academy-id "e-7050" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/be/be4b1ac82a25533e28028ad0aade71e2dd2614c5cd302d777a90a9f16be845c1.svg)\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda=3.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/df/dfddfc4e82f21cd490aff75bbdc6fbece98cd05a5cc70a1148492697faa8741a.svg)\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda=3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/92/92c6c6be816658e065ebba1079a2c7fa031cc9137ffa8dec7bf6e498e69195f3.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/75/75524e426e894f50985740367c96f128dfef1e216ae6969a529889cf22803bc7.svg)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1  \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}t e^{\\lambda t} +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7-3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/bb/bb892c2fd7b1a2873b9a7427d95599f67fbd10e47ec6fd2cb377b9f6f45f4185.svg)\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_1 =\\dfrac{1}{2} - 2 w_2 .\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_2$ is a free variable. Setting $w_2 = 0,$  we get $w_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n![](images/46/4696fe4ee3fd46a639fb465c10aa2ac44e608a0cf59ca6c1a210eb4861b3dd5b.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/05/0525d47967c203832ff4deea0931e007cc7127f24d248cd8265765ad95708367.svg)\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/48/48da1d81a447aa78c3ffa68ad5f089bc0d8e4bac5fa489ddad2747055b0f2a99.svg) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-2(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(4t-3){e}^{3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1635bde-7b58-54e7-895f-915db0b50cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(2+t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300848} {:answer/id #uuid "baf6c3b6-ca24-5611-96b8-993adf0129e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{4t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300898} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fd8283-591d-5f67-b3cd-17968f78cdfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300945} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a4780e-6ce1-5e3e-8873-06f09e1e4957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301080} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4364a0-440b-5051-a349-f5a8feb58d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300945} :answer-field/id #uuid "f52d6758-b968-51b9-a5a2-6e03ab7af674" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302009}] :question/id #uuid "9d755d77-3b2c-5d58-97ac-ef5c72049848" :question/math-academy-id "q-325135" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4-4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+2{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1abe58c-4e50-51b8-9764-580811dbae8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3t} \\\\ -e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155279} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f3ce66-b7ea-50e4-992a-e44e9beef85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2t} \\\\ 2 - 3e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155280} {:answer/id #uuid "f07f1743-746c-5ba5-8926-ae2979e4a9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-2t} \\\\ -e^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155281} {:answer/id #uuid "07ac43d1-9f6e-55bd-9e5e-8a2d48141b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-2t} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155282} {:answer/id #uuid "26196474-9868-51db-8001-6c006ffe5d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155283}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bb8695b8-5480-5a53-bb34-879ee0234397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155278}] :question/id #uuid "61675324-bdd9-52fc-bedb-c3bbf2b76beb" :question/math-academy-id "q-325136" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $λ =-2$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ca2d32a-4c04-50ff-91f0-8046260807a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300584} {:answer/id #uuid "d2289300-75a9-5b84-84a6-d44ebc08ff8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301009} {:answer/id #uuid "e9eb9a48-bf69-59f2-b28c-d145d8739241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301120} {:answer/id #uuid "f182e5a2-a9f3-573e-9415-618ee51f716c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301154} {:answer/id #uuid "f8829e7a-583b-577a-8af0-a6321d9e75f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (2-12t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301154} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8123425-b2ad-5be3-af0f-d40f08f7cc7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301873}] :question/id #uuid "6648c16d-423d-571d-89b3-28f10f5f61b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-326238" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3-(-3) & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3-(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-6{w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{{c}_{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-12t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b833ac-6e99-5f04-9aea-02a1d1abf1cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-6t){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299983} {:answer/id #uuid "30e58490-02de-57c1-a235-c5f21bc66f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300197} {:answer/id #uuid "3f187179-b0f8-5d94-91aa-4179df6e4772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300272} {:answer/id #uuid "848d9602-7869-51e2-9531-be656bfae476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(-3-12t){e}^{5t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300623} {:answer/id #uuid "dea94c08-d4a8-50f4-b269-2013de24496f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301066} :answer-field/id #uuid "9741ff31-5e13-50b4-8cd7-e54eafbd4045" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301713}] :question/id #uuid "26bb41ab-79e8-5c9f-95c2-ae20713f031a" :question/math-academy-id "q-326239" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\\,t{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130d3616-3670-5a5c-b8da-00c9c23fef02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345514} {:answer/id #uuid "163b9c8a-95d6-5825-8c03-376d7d06d2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345547} {:answer/id #uuid "21ddf266-a502-52e7-9722-392dfd92faaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1+2t){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345652} {:answer/id #uuid "3615743c-9a59-5828-a5f9-76c3c49cabeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345819} {:answer/id #uuid "7345187f-4860-54a2-b08f-2475beaa1d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346369} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1a6eed3-c8a4-5660-aaac-2d37375ee8a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347889}] :question/id #uuid "608190d5-e5cd-5f82-85c0-726eb6ec9bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-80608" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1-1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}-(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42865a1d-e7dd-5742-a905-26015fcbdb2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349050} {:answer/id #uuid "690b864e-4301-536f-abb4-d98819a36a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349415} {:answer/id #uuid "96e30ebf-f753-5f2c-bfda-1443e5566cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349801} {:answer/id #uuid "9a777a2e-88d9-547b-9226-0f78646dae73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349824} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bfb469-6607-5051-a5c7-d072ad71b3c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349801} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaac654c-f8f2-5836-8ce9-667c860921c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351124}] :question/id #uuid "594f8d9b-4d22-56cb-9100-eca1eb140e4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80927" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2+2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2+2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}. \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bebbc4e-9f09-5a63-8a78-66c4c71c9f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394080} {:answer/id #uuid "123d83fc-2ef6-5c06-bf82-70606697e4b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-2t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394081} {:answer/id #uuid "9c62ecd3-428e-522b-87ab-bab07f5373dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394082} {:answer/id #uuid "c68c4d6c-df9d-5223-85aa-092175501fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394083} {:answer/id #uuid "8e997a41-67be-53d5-a641-39bf9d7812b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-2t){e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394082} :answer-field/id #uuid "6748730c-ed4f-573c-8760-a302d2f3d2a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394079}] :question/id #uuid "6e25df6c-8786-547c-9562-9085a352e6d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80928" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-7+1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5+1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{w}_{1}-4{w}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ 9{w}_{1}+6{w}_{2} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+5(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832a4fcb-62eb-56e2-9f41-a3e29a5ed8ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394094} {:answer/id #uuid "cb48d995-b143-517c-bc67-98f2954c6043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394095} {:answer/id #uuid "900135ff-25f0-5ee7-acc6-8b3908232894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394096} {:answer/id #uuid "f871941e-3ad1-5c73-8823-a810d976d907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394097} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4f60eb-8be3-5479-8850-71f5a129a743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394094} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9abc986-3e86-5236-8582-73591221120f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394093}] :question/id #uuid "238c50de-f678-5486-9e97-0de67f7c80f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80932" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =7.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10-7 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4-7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{w}_{1}+{w}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -9{w}_{1}-3{w}_{2} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{w}_{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{7t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{7t}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2466ac54-a28c-5e81-8865-e657fdfdddf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529657} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8a7671-a4f9-5cb6-aad5-295efc3dd7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+2t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2t-1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529768} {:answer/id #uuid "6015db17-0453-5d51-9f6c-9b63c750a7e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529813} {:answer/id #uuid "7d81a220-377a-5acf-8093-4fcf21b38c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529896} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ea50a5-4a7b-5fa7-80c3-71388673c51f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+\\frac{t}{2}){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2t-1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529813} :answer-field/id #uuid "15c8b6c9-4d90-5de4-bd62-0285c561ccb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530232}] :question/id #uuid "4a2a9a41-3861-52b6-aa0d-2940105366a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-80616" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-1{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31af8209-d6f7-5861-bb8f-000ec6cd874e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}(t-1){e}^{5t} \\\\ 2{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529690} {:answer/id #uuid "6bed8f55-cb39-5a3b-86c2-5cd8c26c256c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529854} {:answer/id #uuid "a90db176-5114-5ca8-a22e-ebcadf82891a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}(t-1) \\\\ (2-t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529998} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ce04dc-14c5-5800-b05b-94c7bf45b6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{5t} \\\\ (t+2){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530038} {:answer/id #uuid "d8570b37-63eb-524c-ba0e-1892354b0a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529854} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d46ab32-dd48-56fe-af69-855cd332f49c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530314}] :question/id #uuid "a4894a4b-386a-5db4-8e7b-0e56f6d08b5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80617" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5-5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c30eff0d-ffd1-5d17-bc6f-a345783aae21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1+t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530067} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4d4ff6-2575-5aa5-adaf-1dfeb10d5b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530102} {:answer/id #uuid "db46ec5a-9287-56d8-98f8-2de1ee93eb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530121} {:answer/id #uuid "e582a2bf-4e2c-5f80-aa69-4386efa886fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530155} {:answer/id #uuid "e930397d-0699-53ec-a3a5-3a49c82b1c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530161} :answer-field/id #uuid "80a2d0fe-c382-5881-8e02-d1c86d71cb09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530318}] :question/id #uuid "d67f8554-75f7-5018-9643-79d6cf0e83b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80929" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-3.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4+3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2818 :topic/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116106} :activity/id #uuid "c73bd47d-c95f-5782-913d-11a66b5cf13b" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116490 :tutorial/content "Matrices can be used to represent systems of equations, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=5 \\\\ 4x\\text{ }-\\text{ }5y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\times 2$ system like the one above, we put the coefficients of the system into a $2\\times 2$ matrix, called the **coefficient matrix**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEach row has the coefficients of one of the equations in the system, and each column consists of the coefficients of one particular variable.\nWe also have the $2\\times 1$ column vectors\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}$ consists of each constant on the right side of the system and $\\mathbf{x}$ contains the variables.\nWith these matrices, we can write our system as $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ or\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can write any system of two equations with two variables in this form.\nJust to check that the above equation is true, let's multiply out the left-hand side to see if it equals the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x+3y \\\\ 4x\\text{ }-\\text{ }5y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =b\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d49da49a-c3f8-5718-91a9-4adc236155d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3048 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f7a59c72-2c98-5267-942e-326053020128" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce2e21aa-f89d-5d10-b815-c66a2b46ef5c"} :step/id #uuid "b1ea43d1-62f0-5ea5-b504-4e92139c4783" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116108}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f746a1-ea9e-5136-90d6-d4dee1306c17"} :step/id #uuid "8395e7bd-de36-5136-8556-30a9d038a483" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115233 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2300a1-f31e-5a24-889a-6f45e1e8647b"} :step/id #uuid "896b2689-9de5-527f-a925-513c7675ce5c"}] :activity/title "Representing 2x2 Systems of Equations Using a Matrix Product" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115475}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050463 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115231 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115062 :question/id #uuid "87bf279f-9f78-54cb-b9c3-e1bf1c5426ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-4505" :question/problem "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=5 \\\\ -5x+6y &=-8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation. The variable $y$ does not appear in the first equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+0y &=5 \\\\ -5x+6y &=-8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n![](images/cd/cdadd8a9c68f58c4d8434e658d4acffeba524850f361fcd3d6fe3524b3d781a1.png)\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n![](images/35/35104266eb5a086bef0536640ec1310532cc136a251049a1adcfe4418ef0ef89.png)\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n![](images/aa/aa4de061ca95088763e7e5fec289dbd2e5bd298e8986ef6660617ea0ba227821.png)\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ becomes\n\n![](images/10/10781c0f9183c793e3f2de287ede937e764d942767ecc9b64942dc338c1e1307.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce2e21aa-f89d-5d10-b815-c66a2b46ef5c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac702230-e0b6-5149-946f-efd4c6240c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111962} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d139bd-6d1e-5f66-a303-7e6b7a0e51de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111963} {:answer/id #uuid "d2848f95-94e5-5ab3-a01f-412463cb5507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111964} {:answer/id #uuid "02c5947d-7aa5-5ba5-a47b-e026a2989d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111965} {:answer/id #uuid "70c3eeea-3b68-5006-bd47-787332cbacad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111966}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a5747825-fbaf-5c36-b201-e23205e21435" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111961}] :question/id #uuid "ebdaa38a-80f3-5181-996b-ca5c169aa5a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-11979" :question/problem "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n${3x + 5y = 4; x - 7y =-4$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a223b0ba-cc0b-5488-b008-a5a33b2c2c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111968} {:answer/id #uuid "da2e69a5-cf6f-57d2-8d67-6bb25c167d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111969} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5978af-c54e-596d-9642-e6db91c6d113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111970} {:answer/id #uuid "31eefa3e-ed3a-5aff-86ff-364467e08fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111971} {:answer/id #uuid "5eca5f73-c48b-5682-bc09-1cce81fbb4cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111972}] :answer-field/id #uuid "67908db4-252d-5747-9818-1cf87b96d68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186111967}] :question/id #uuid "1ae858d6-0d70-54f7-a023-67b6c0b13f2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-12288" :question/problem "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n${2y = 3; x = 0$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad42806-a1fb-502b-92c9-cc550a2947d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263104} {:answer/id #uuid "3814d838-f67b-5fd0-9cf1-5bd3b8068e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263506} {:answer/id #uuid "70300fb3-4714-53a0-81a4-d324647570c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263972} {:answer/id #uuid "a40dd9f9-794b-5250-b118-912c1bcea248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264398} {:answer/id #uuid "c23a5f0c-9b6e-5b61-bcc9-719659fcd17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264687} :answer-field/id #uuid "1393e60c-9a9d-558f-82b5-5e2a716a7edd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265276}] :question/id #uuid "5438297a-3242-553b-b76b-9231f3aaa360" :question/math-academy-id "q-130501" :question/problem "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y &=4 \\\\ x-y &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation, all the variables are on the left-hand side, and all the constants are on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y &=4 \\\\ x-y &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5799816e-9719-5798-9c9a-f732bf85395d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297918} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e09e8b7-c1e7-5293-a3b0-a64b67e11230" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "054e6b51-eb57-5c1b-9a70-ba76b85003af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297579}] :answ