igned}\\right.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422854} {:answer/id #uuid "be15d0f0-70f8-5910-afd6-1af63be69956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+5y &=9 \\\\ y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5y &=9 \\\\ x &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422854} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c4d849c-e24c-5150-9286-220ece393a3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400486}] :question/id #uuid "a1162f6f-3b06-5edc-926f-fe3ac4ca244f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130528" :question/problem "What is the system of equations represented by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0x+5y &=9 \\\\ x+0y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5y &=9 \\\\ x &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b0257b-5aa5-5961-8c79-b270612973ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ x-4y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403497} {:answer/id #uuid "efe4f88d-8258-5d39-be46-855c1e2c58e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ -4x+y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403498} {:answer/id #uuid "7cea8dbe-3578-5886-bfe4-f51b25347ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ x-4y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403499} {:answer/id #uuid "aba3c242-ef16-5d89-8d0a-32ce47931725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=5 \\\\ x-4y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403500} {:answer/id #uuid "777783df-e9bd-5dfd-9c9a-d3ccfc310aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ x-3y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ x-4y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403497} :answer-field/id #uuid "796fd7cb-3aaa-51b5-a9d2-6f0f250be044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403496}] :question/id #uuid "7f74fa12-0053-502d-b17f-669ab25e05d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-71854" :question/problem "What is the system of equations represented by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0x-y &=0 \\\\ -3x+12y &=-15.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ x-4y &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Matrix Product as a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1729 :topic/title "Representing 2x2 Systems of Equations Using a Matrix Product"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125416} :activity/id #uuid "b63f5657-355a-5f0a-a214-2065b7a76d74" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125789 :tutorial/content "The **invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvalues** states the following:\n\n*An $n \\times n$ matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$* \n\nAs a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, if we are given that\n\n$$\n\\lambda^3-\\lambda^2-2\\lambda=0\n$$\n\nis the characteristic equation of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A,$ then $A$ must be singular (not invertible) since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "1e561ab8-38f8-5ec1-a285-6559f35aa7cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3569 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "babad562-3d88-57e5-a6c7-a8b53bec4956" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125417}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282"} :step/id #uuid "46208d38-6fce-5121-8f5a-512e318a7731" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125418 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d"} :step/id #uuid "4880d3b1-8971-5b5f-8646-d786ae6c89fb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338"} :step/id #uuid "e492707b-f646-51d5-b4f4-34c984760081" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125418}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124751} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488313} :activity/id #uuid "382bc946-87c1-5af9-a714-bd8312897100" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362257 :question/id #uuid "68bc418d-6421-5050-b933-2a80a591867e"} :step/id #uuid "0a855166-560a-5030-8b45-18d477914230" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488246 :question/id #uuid "a57f1e60-3a1f-5809-920d-a2e3527a6835"} :step/id #uuid "44b1d683-1553-59db-aadd-0a5e6069911a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362248 :question/id #uuid "b1b3df2e-4878-5f07-9682-4c09eb922bca"} :step/id #uuid "6a18fb35-57db-5780-914c-83b1dbef88e1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362252 :question/id #uuid "f1c970e5-75bc-5ce7-946f-a4ae8b32b111"} :step/id #uuid "901bfe44-d04a-5966-874a-7a238c9dc1ba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362254 :question/id #uuid "651bcec7-c2ce-52d1-b1e2-d8f7e6384008"} :step/id #uuid "f5e9d767-80a5-5062-a420-ab68984dcbd8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488290}}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487555}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050470 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124337 :question/id #uuid "efccacfd-9595-5315-86c7-de9fefd8ed1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5497" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\nI. $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n\nII. $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n\nIII. $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$" :question/worked-solution "According to the invertible matrix theorem, if $A$ is invertible, then $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e0031aa-3dbc-5f68-93e0-bb2ed010e74d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123027} {:answer/id #uuid "d0573792-ad45-544b-94fd-b9d9148f8136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123028} {:answer/id #uuid "e56fa8f8-674f-55cb-bba7-ff19538337c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123029} {:answer/id #uuid "f84d1c10-aa15-5508-bc23-2f0ebdf5ce51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123030} {:answer/id #uuid "54341bfe-9ab7-5883-9acb-5a53daf0e5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123031} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e79e35d-b84d-52e4-966a-93f9f1835803" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123026}] :question/id #uuid "e5a631d9-8e9c-5aa8-b5b9-8e752a0ab9bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-37423" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\nI. $\\lambda (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -1)=0$\n\nII. ${\\lambda }^{3}+3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +4=0$\n\nIII. ${\\lambda }^{3}-5{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda =0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47e1bc98-e7e4-52f8-984d-07090fcce509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448608} {:answer/id #uuid "bd61a0f6-b4d4-5449-b479-f5e34f173f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448609} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cef80fb-6ab7-552e-906b-93d05bf07abc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab6b7cce-8578-54ae-a81c-3138c19f494d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448610} {:answer/id #uuid "4eaa0245-8571-54f0-b959-74a0a10d530d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448611} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c3d2be-b0df-5bbf-8142-98edc40dc42f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449345} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0254d36b-a197-5a46-943b-99f03cefa2b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448612} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbbfda1-4699-5f8e-b698-221bd7d85dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448612} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5503ea3-b219-5b46-b79b-0e271f4f1b90" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449346}] :question/id #uuid "3be4d323-0e25-52c5-8eb8-5afe165a1c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-37523" :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda =0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =3$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-6(3) & =0 \\\\ 27-9-18 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d4ff535-a1f0-5ce6-8b0f-0685c923aebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464138} {:answer/id #uuid "e9853f79-3460-5bbc-b09c-1486f81f69b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464138} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9e19efd-b193-5d8c-951e-06d2594d3c69" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d591cdfe-787c-561e-b1ab-96a237381b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464140} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd54853-c89a-5086-aedb-4b0387aa7aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464141} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d9f0ad-5dbc-52a9-a14d-c0423c3659b8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d063335-7f55-523f-94d2-bc87bd49b8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464142} {:answer/id #uuid "c08d56be-7940-5e02-8c76-d6c91fd825db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464142} :answer-field/id #uuid "708f3344-420a-5f82-a998-358eeabd9c9b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465005}] :question/id #uuid "27c4d64e-3a6c-52f2-bb70-03acd0cf6bbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140834" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n- **I.** $\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$\n- **II.** $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$\n- **III.** $5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b49fd5-f088-5454-be5d-5107c647f0f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449580} {:answer/id #uuid "13e0f499-69e4-5ec1-b4f2-1ad1e4f28df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449581} :answer-field/id #uuid "272bcebc-fe8b-57b4-ae53-626fe72f2b23" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450744} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e17838a-706f-5603-a10d-92de20c57c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449582} {:answer/id #uuid "0ab54959-e3f9-5e7c-b65b-3f4c44ab857d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449582} :answer-field/id #uuid "c399833e-a33e-5bf1-abf9-d826969c4585" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62e97b3f-deab-5976-8ff6-07262066e30a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449584} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3e26df-93cc-5b9e-a97e-f3c98599c684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449585} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1e96681-263d-592f-94b0-d87293b9e481" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450746}] :question/id #uuid "1812a9f8-6cf3-5d8f-b5c7-1adf1d688a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-140835" :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 16-12-4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the characteristic equation, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7f09b1c-9964-5ae6-9322-bf6de2b57681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467398} {:answer/id #uuid "dd858fdf-4fbe-5af0-b7f7-3d32c5fc5ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467399} :answer-field/id #uuid "e939d814-ae4b-507f-b423-19079a26ac60" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468420} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2758e74-ea85-5f56-a8cf-c1056f59c3d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467400} {:answer/id #uuid "e83bbd17-0d14-5570-83ce-8018039ac54b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467401} :answer-field/id #uuid "6089e5b4-5b31-5cc3-8e8f-3c2b0ef217f0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3adb0444-ee54-507e-bc74-75995133eade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467402} {:answer/id #uuid "e35df19c-c12d-5884-9bab-6220a82bac4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467402} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfa1370c-c573-5558-8a76-57bd2125c1c9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468422}] :question/id #uuid "26a6234c-3080-57fe-a159-1f4332413072" :question/math-academy-id "q-37527" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n- **I.** ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0$\n- **II.** $(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +5)=0$\n- **III.** ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda =0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could  be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "436bf24b-bdf6-5bd1-bf14-e293249ddd46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452431} {:answer/id #uuid "363b7103-4c5b-566a-8bb6-bb6db139809a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452432} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed94cd18-02ea-5163-8871-843e8d20bacd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddb395a3-0026-5fe2-8672-0d8a776661b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452433} {:answer/id #uuid "ad49d154-32c7-51a1-9c40-23951e669167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452433} :answer-field/id #uuid "85f07249-c02b-5278-9c34-c03c810c79e2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453064} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b316a2be-90ba-5f03-af54-19e6e2c49f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452435} {:answer/id #uuid "4beb83e8-81ac-5a00-996b-7891230847a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452436} :answer-field/id #uuid "75b5380c-d718-5a65-a9f5-9759ac621a6b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453065}] :question/id #uuid "2a9eef89-f664-5ecc-a524-f84c3e33cc1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97871" :question/problem "A matrix $B$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n- **III.** $B$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =1$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 1-2+1 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (2)^{4}-2(2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 16-8+1 & =0 \\\\ 9 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60987df5-8c3c-54ae-838c-cd701fb442dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471789} {:answer/id #uuid "8f001032-df56-5a30-a156-b43b6ff775fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471790} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a388f01-4a46-5643-acde-373829d0e415" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472400} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c598417-054a-57eb-b548-de0798c7d570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471791} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8aaebf-d279-5803-83a3-bd17d7899f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471791} :answer-field/id #uuid "297b34f9-4160-5f88-8408-a21af344a053" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472401} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55ae7047-3b7e-51d5-9be1-7e2f66eecf5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471793} {:answer/id #uuid "d9fb77f4-4254-5c73-953b-7f787e0f61d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471794} :answer-field/id #uuid "74391771-4e6a-57d2-9134-21c7fb4ea916" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472402}] :question/id #uuid "f94c3089-795c-58b5-a020-124bd7b98fb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97859" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ non-invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n- **I.** ${\\lambda }^{3}-4\\lambda =0$\n- **II.** ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +4=0$\n- **III.** $\\lambda (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1)=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is non-invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ must be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a10545-2e6a-5410-8600-0c239ab36b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477811} {:answer/id #uuid "6a09fece-7821-5290-ba7c-33c8642353c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477951} {:answer/id #uuid "75d3c0ba-ecc5-531e-a32d-4aad91e28a39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477982} {:answer/id #uuid "8b187d27-74f7-5cce-9dc4-b36648121255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478018} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb064b3-880e-5a8f-a066-a9c976e3909b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478018} :answer-field/id #uuid "e13459e6-807d-540e-845c-f7ab2efc8cf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478349}] :question/id #uuid "bc0c0d74-29c3-5a54-b40b-f1888e3dbce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97861" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $C?$\n\nI. ${\\lambda }^{4}+2{\\lambda }^{3}+3{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda =0$\n\nII. $\\lambda (\\lambda -2)^{2}(\\lambda +3)=0$\n\nIII. ${\\lambda }^{4}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+4=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $C$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $C$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could  be the characteristic equation of $C.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b3b01ef-bfa0-55f1-9056-f3e4b2324723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551764} {:answer/id #uuid "450ebeb7-8c1d-5bf2-8007-6ecd245f79f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551834} {:answer/id #uuid "5bcdffd3-a42f-5c79-9e93-d65d68d85a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551886} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d6f1d4-ab94-5f86-afdb-f2fac5be826b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552200} {:answer/id #uuid "e9952794-53e3-52a3-bce8-c59c81cb3b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551834} :answer-field/id #uuid "da5d63d6-fbb6-5332-9094-308af65e75ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552380}] :question/id #uuid "e7077a60-6a06-585e-8213-5137d377235a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97869" :question/problem "A matrix $B$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda =0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n\nII. $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nIII. $B$ is non-invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (2)^{3}+2(2)^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 8+8-2 & =0 \\\\ 14 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is non-invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124338 :question/id #uuid "d7619d9b-e5d7-55e5-96a8-0d05b3532620" :question/math-academy-id "e-5498" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n\nII. $B +4I$ is singular\n\nIII. $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/60/6031351ba073c1da6df2d7e96ef1764eff8d6e15d1d1c388e06edf68dc6e9083.svg)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc28d24-2966-5404-9657-12db361e583f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448834} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb9ebe2-2b9f-520b-ae5c-3a3b086533b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448834} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7a49a0-682e-5485-8704-c21a8df81e3a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449457} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8f791e7-bb86-5a9f-b7a4-d3cc7968a9bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448836} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d857bb-3ddc-59a7-a9e1-b62a1bf5bbc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448837} :answer-field/id #uuid "979415cb-cf88-5d43-b613-b0e7364d110e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449458} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4cb6286-7e1d-5d71-95bf-438c63766041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448838} {:answer/id #uuid "c7255ac2-2e51-5f09-8fcc-64d6f0d0e25e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448839} :answer-field/id #uuid "4da59e2a-2004-5754-a752-61aa8752b4c9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449459}] :question/id #uuid "bef513f1-2d4e-54ff-9389-039c04a6d703" :question/math-academy-id "q-97051" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A+2I)=2$\n- **II.** $A+2I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+2I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+2I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A+2I$ is singular, then $(A+2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c0e1ab-fdc2-5768-b1ef-fb121a44d8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477509} {:answer/id #uuid "de380f74-3fca-519b-867a-f0e67008d2e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477510} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3258524-fdc6-53c6-bbcc-76e01ddb6edc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef0ecbe8-cc59-5492-90d0-3d962ab63e98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477511} {:answer/id #uuid "679f3b09-0a22-58de-9792-601c8d391ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477511} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ee16b1d-ffdb-5b63-b376-cf42223f4b30" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477725} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8170e32c-1075-5f7b-b9bc-b57335a6d09d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477513} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8d7101-86f6-5fbb-8883-6d5c4b920ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477513} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e06b12-0373-5de0-95a6-c2db0f62e210" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477726}] :question/id #uuid "72b961d6-3f11-54e6-b3ea-21ca6cd541b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97974" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(C-5I)=3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $\\text{Null}(C-5I)={0}$\n- **II.** $C-5I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(C-5I) & =3-\\text{rank}(C-5I) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(C-5I)={0}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C-5I)=0,$ we obtain that $C-5I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=C-5I$ is non-singular, then $(C-5I)x=0$ has only zero solution.  As a result, $\\lambda =5$ can not be an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8814293-c8a9-578f-8657-39a79ba1371c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449592} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfdb8f8-d8cb-528e-b9a4-cc0c1b63c6e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449593} :answer-field/id #uuid "4178ebcf-70fd-5899-ba5f-d287bb465aff" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450750} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07fbd334-3cb4-5c97-bc5d-e7ad07664145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449594} {:answer/id #uuid "86749cb9-992d-5952-84f4-2774168aaffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449594} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ed7cb0-5034-527d-8e0b-18cfb88e3287" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450751} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c3ee31-dad8-510e-a6f6-7b0ff0870e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449596} {:answer/id #uuid "52e36725-146c-55d1-82ce-88952ae9ecda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449597} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b37f56-b08e-5b30-9546-ef7378db28c7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450752}] :question/id #uuid "f77ae0b2-0cbc-5c99-a8dc-82a79dae8fdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140840" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-7I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A-7I$ is singular\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)=0$\n- **III.** $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A-7I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-7I) & =2-\\text{rank}(A-7I) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A-7I$ is singular, then $(A-7I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b4be9e5-5d17-516f-93ec-642cf35049d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449598} {:answer/id #uuid "cab9c958-3a80-540b-ab7a-187b9352bd82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449599} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e69b620-3713-58e6-9c80-9c97e40a0bac" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450753} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f4ce6ec-b2f5-5058-9d0d-7b33a69016c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449600} {:answer/id #uuid "adb445f6-8d41-53c4-b1c0-49499e68f169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449601} :answer-field/id #uuid "2734be2f-47b8-53e8-a929-15f253facda9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ccfdef-4d8c-5a2b-a0e4-4e9f919059db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449602} {:answer/id #uuid "c783651d-7745-524f-98a6-8e6ba3438853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449602} :answer-field/id #uuid "60e7c511-421d-56c7-ab16-331aa5063b36" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450755}] :question/id #uuid "78a8d2b4-93c9-5ecc-ada3-6436c09c3461" :question/math-academy-id "q-140842" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A+8I$ is singular\n- **II.** $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(A+8I)=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+8I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that if $M=A+8I$ is singular, then $(A+8I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+8I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+8I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "775c219f-a459-55cb-a155-865e3fee7ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452467} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffb7501-a137-566a-82b1-7897016fb94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452468} :answer-field/id #uuid "365204e9-984b-5368-9d39-59ebec92b9e6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453081} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fe10ad-b6fd-51a0-ae35-69c2a8fbad6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452469} {:answer/id #uuid "082ed9ac-ae1b-5302-8e14-5b74ae93b90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452469} :answer-field/id #uuid "470374fb-21bf-5e7b-9721-41f5a726ab31" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b81eebb0-7957-5576-828c-99384c50a06a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452471} {:answer/id #uuid "b516c0cf-2ba1-5ba9-8852-4382e9ac3ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452471} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8bd4bfb-634d-5ae5-aa62-0dafe1569a4e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453083}] :question/id #uuid "7aa7ebc3-80d5-541f-813e-64c8db0cc171" :question/math-academy-id "q-97962" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}$\n- **II.** $A-3I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A-3I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A-3I) \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is non-singular, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2cedf5f-ea78-598b-8fea-86eb4930a16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452473} {:answer/id #uuid "ac501363-d562-542c-8971-fa619b64fb93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452474} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed2e22e-171c-554d-a637-c726d3aaa309" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "941dacde-a17c-5765-910f-5bc4839a1386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452475} {:answer/id #uuid "5f487188-0cec-5f38-9e34-e190f507bb5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452476} :answer-field/id #uuid "611e09b5-b47a-5519-9300-6bc0ece8a2f0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0044e830-172d-574e-b097-e1ebe25e1adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452477} {:answer/id #uuid "4200c14b-bbe6-5725-adaa-903da877df9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452477} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef6a8d9-228c-5e0b-8e7a-462c8a409290" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453086}] :question/id #uuid "651bcec7-c2ce-52d1-b1e2-d8f7e6384008" :question/math-academy-id "q-97969" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-3I)=4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$\n- **II.** $A-3I$ is invertible\n- **III.** $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(A-3I) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is invertible, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d33d9c39-fb68-5072-8e1d-d5100f882d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452479} {:answer/id #uuid "31f89fd5-7b10-551c-bc20-cccf8c2d412f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452479} :answer-field/id #uuid "51a700a6-056d-5fbf-9c15-802b0bdd40bd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453087} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f33abab6-7da2-5b60-9d2f-3f60b47ce8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452481} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9de0a6-d221-54ed-ad32-0d48a983af48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452482} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ec61757-aa1d-5552-94ae-87ef06754a16" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9364d49b-0c70-5f53-9afc-58a6465fdfe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452483} {:answer/id #uuid "bb43d0d6-435a-59ca-b0d6-dd54c6d2f55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452483} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d70d8de-2041-5147-b2df-9b23496268ca" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453089}] :question/id #uuid "fb578155-2783-5751-8e62-1deb89778f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-97970" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(B-I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(B-I)={R}^{3}$\n- **II.** $B-I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =1$ is not an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(B-I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(B-I) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(B-I)\\ne 3,$ according to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(B-I)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B-I$ is singular, then $(B-I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2408efc0-effa-5d1a-95bf-4e5888eb4e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452485} {:answer/id #uuid "478fc76b-1ffb-5eba-9444-fb9a5daab721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452485} :answer-field/id #uuid "298cd9f6-0f4b-5d91-b1e2-380da3c049cf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453090} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890097e8-1b87-5c8b-80f8-73adc10117b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452487} {:answer/id #uuid "194e8931-2c02-5f42-b19b-625e045d7857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452487} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a5a7b14-0732-5180-838e-5db043472ec8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453091} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88db7284-406b-5896-82c0-024eb4192579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452489} {:answer/id #uuid "35417b18-69fa-55aa-bd77-4e154b50f8f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452490} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d73097d-9167-5ae9-ac40-5d49927706d1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453092}] :question/id #uuid "89915cc7-c25f-5711-a62f-10d7e3f1b8b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97972" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(C-2I)={R}^{2}$\n- **II.** $C-2I$ is non-singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(C-2I) & =2-\\text{nullity}(C-2I) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C-2I$ is singular, then $(C-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4ccb0fb-57b3-54cc-9a0e-7b3b2ec086c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452491} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0736bf-0992-5540-92e4-4db3dc860d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452491} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7a2ad23-7a6f-5d92-beff-9522a7a1c6e4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453093} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4c9d99e-8bcf-530d-a35b-845c74343724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452493} {:answer/id #uuid "b94d0759-6f74-5338-954d-fc2d4ed8aa4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452494} :answer-field/id #uuid "eabb1107-ac4d-5f76-9ad8-0708cf4bdbd2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453094} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee5ed75-acac-56ef-98ed-57d5822de8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452495} {:answer/id #uuid "537989db-6a16-5fcc-aa37-3d4aa93c947c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452496} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f83e84-79e0-5f94-a3f0-7b675d59f092" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453095}] :question/id #uuid "68bc418d-6421-5050-b933-2a80a591867e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97975" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(C+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Null}(C+3I)={0}$\n- **II.** $C+3I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(C+3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(C+3I) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(C+3I)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C+3I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C+3I$ is singular, then $(C+3I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f28abe1-7ce7-5fcd-800c-5355d28b4dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454274} {:answer/id #uuid "975b6d1f-67e4-5ad0-a60c-f212c150bba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454275} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e4ad4b-797b-5e1c-957c-e3b4e3622e5b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b268ef17-aa66-5222-b880-47a780072089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454276} {:answer/id #uuid "98e68f38-5fa0-5696-a788-a965baf32116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454276} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0d8911-05a3-5867-85d8-d03e8d8a263a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454888} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9e6411f-f645-554b-849c-9e1f5f16f32f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454278} {:answer/id #uuid "0df6ff7c-41ab-51b6-a2ff-1f6813596ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454279} :answer-field/id #uuid "8031e5cb-78c7-579e-9e68-cf112703c2a4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454889}] :question/id #uuid "a33206bb-7969-5524-a745-290fffeff16f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97967" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B-2I)=3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)=1$\n- **II.** $B-2I$ is non-singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B-2I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B-2I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B-2I$ is singular, then $(B-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3506dab1-62ad-546f-b8d5-2e43cdacdb38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477838} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9ffb8b-392e-547a-b8dd-0ab8af442d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477886} {:answer/id #uuid "91eb4fef-125c-5602-af85-46700f3acf65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478023} {:answer/id #uuid "9b33856e-57bb-575b-a78c-287c2948dabb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478034} {:answer/id #uuid "c64ada4d-645e-5dde-adf5-9d7a1b8e974e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478034} :answer-field/id #uuid "754fcbaf-4a94-546a-a360-fa1f3050ad24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478306}] :question/id #uuid "e4997f39-9351-5840-b1b4-1804fe6c9f0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97971" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $B+3I$ is singular\n\nIII. $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+3I) & =2-\\text{rank}(B+3I) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B+3I)=0,$ we obtain that $B+3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B+3I$ is non-singular, then $(B+3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124417 :question/id #uuid "107369d4-980d-5d73-85ea-8fcf522b96f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8606" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n\nII. $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{ \\mathbf{0}\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/08/084da4d2f1ef5b0a2359a9a6b7582daf57f619842453fd911233eb004862b941.svg)\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb1fef96-a4b5-56a3-ae12-1ad088781515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448702} {:answer/id #uuid "c988276e-ae24-58e1-b3c3-71288a68bb61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448703} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cfef4ec-bf41-5b26-a871-8eaa14e4c137" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fab0312d-2e7a-525e-ba6d-532165dc1bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448704} {:answer/id #uuid "46330574-7aae-5314-b20d-1471b582f872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448704} :answer-field/id #uuid "004390c1-de50-56c3-839a-de91fd3c41a3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449392} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7cdfa98-4044-5fa7-991a-cdd2d17905fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448706} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ebdbcd-7ff6-5904-beff-00069d922192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448706} :answer-field/id #uuid "872a7bbb-a3ff-52ad-be3b-b42dbd804cf8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449393}] :question/id #uuid "c0e0c0ef-5198-55d8-9aeb-10b338580777" :question/math-academy-id "q-47568" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A-3I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-3I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-3I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A-3I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-3I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beea7466-c41d-589d-865f-48504bd7909f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448852} {:answer/id #uuid "155e2c10-fe31-5b66-8f91-aef1c4a7653e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448852} :answer-field/id #uuid "14c928b0-156c-536e-bed2-6f5b5e4401fc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46813a85-d060-55df-bb6c-d675e5cbd606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448854} {:answer/id #uuid "cd88e49f-fa58-5c5b-9e14-f03a5d794525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448854} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2f9766b-60ff-519d-951a-b93e45549f26" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449467} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92227e0d-7a0b-5d15-9e0d-3258bf7187f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448856} {:answer/id #uuid "069779b3-0176-5660-81d6-ff6ef90ecab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448857} :answer-field/id #uuid "46bf0043-aa6e-51a8-975c-6599d0da478c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449468}] :question/id #uuid "b68a0678-9348-57e6-b4b3-d9e83bab09ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-97961" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A+I$ is a non-singular matrix\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A+I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II is false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+I)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31698bf4-835e-5391-bd94-e5338ea6725e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449586} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3381fd-405f-5c82-9e7e-171f8dc5dbf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449587} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d2e4de-00be-5228-ac60-63ff9be610ee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5889c469-8246-5988-a4b6-18db86a78f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449588} {:answer/id #uuid "4c685a84-65d1-5256-b2c2-aa1ebe137a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449588} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dca00bd-3230-521f-ad18-be85e3bb3f7f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450748} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea8085ac-e799-5424-8198-147afdc52305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449590} {:answer/id #uuid "42e23434-b855-57e0-a5e5-feab08f636f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449590} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee94435-98e2-554c-8d74-17c95d5c7950" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450749}] :question/id #uuid "47d1848e-d69a-5675-a8fe-fca5e30be264" :question/math-academy-id "q-140838" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+4I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362245 :question/an