 matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(C-5I)\\ne 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-5I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9837a523-42e0-533a-a0dd-b42b2ad407d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452461} {:answer/id #uuid "7952bb3e-1500-544d-a00b-8d59f4c87d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452462} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f4c341a-3666-5cd8-9f18-b1da0ee0573a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdeda81d-4c04-5009-bc27-1a8a0454cebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452463} {:answer/id #uuid "da0e6b14-88e6-54aa-ad41-1dba2bdc0fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452464} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c6c176-24dd-5b33