\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b62692e4-70a9-5b64-b572-8e95101f299d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454262} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f96054-430f-5abb-9738-492912b4513f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454263} :answer-field/id #uuid "52fb6fd8-12c2-5a40-95db-e8d434005870" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454881} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43234e8f-f0ca-5794-8734-537c4540ced4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454264} {:answer/id #uuid "aea27afc-4d53-57d1-a1a6-faf52d441711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454265} :answer-field/id #uuid "f38bf0d7-cb37-587a-8201-a3fd186776a4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454882} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f198704-9c2b-52ba-ab29-315f0f4f7390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454266} {:answer/id #uuid "356b12b2-b30c-58ef-898f-2e04dbb2332f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454267} :answer-field/id #uuid "d51aab6a-c250-5230-a3e0-85d81f4fb26b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454883}] :question/id #uuid "2cf92ef9-ce4a-5cbb-9e9d-9e8a2989e53d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97944" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(B+2I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B+2I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B+2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=B+2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a8febcf-d8f8-5bb1-bd0f-5ea5157901db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454268} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0ab0d9-c27a-5f5c-af64-9667450caa58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454268} :answer-field/id #uuid "8384c379-0806-5ddf-b3d0-e3b82670fa9b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26764f15-21bd-5865-b436-a36b9b696003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454270} {:answer/id #uuid "a796bded-1ab7-53dd-8b24-5a4f06d7d263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454271} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a6693f4-1188-5c06-b9f9-4c671b8a0599" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454885} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f7d45de-1453-5098-a4a6-edffb970d0d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454272} {:answer/id #uuid "89362b6b-2fd1-52b4-a6a5-5b6f4d773b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454273} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ba61e62-3f3b-5078-93dc-7f400ec2f0c3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454886}] :question/id #uuid "f636785f-61ec-504d-b842-394a5a79199b" :question/math-academy-id "q-97947" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(C-2I)x=0$ has only zero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-2I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd0d4fb-280a-550e-a592-ead821179211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487590} {:answer/id #uuid "1e87bc2e-1fa6-5dfe-a12d-f3281ca37a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487628} {:answer/id #uuid "79c00a2a-a33d-50c2-9a06-fe7d080d7095" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487806} {:answer/id #uuid "89763caf-f10d-5fe3-91a2-c8336bf4bcdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487830} {:answer/id #uuid "ff51444c-16eb-5add-9d3a-db4575d4b382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487628} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a75aae-536a-560a-b748-38762d5ef8b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488115}] :question/id #uuid "a57f1e60-3a1f-5809-920d-a2e3527a6835" :question/math-academy-id "q-140839" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(C-5I)x=0$ has only zero solution\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76ca1d1a-500b-5c8f-b9a9-1080653b3645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551945} {:answer/id #uuid "87aab433-5768-5369-9cd7-e68caa9a2906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551995} {:answer/id #uuid "897ed2e3-dd67-5c3e-8731-e0ee00a051a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552000} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee117f9-3d2d-59f9-a54e-2398d64d51da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552048} {:answer/id #uuid "c14a2937-1686-50bc-b058-500572e518ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552126} :answer-field/id #uuid "652c36ee-302a-55e4-b7a5-3d103599f696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552310}] :question/id #uuid "a1882648-2096-59b1-b0c6-167402b1eeb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97895" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(A-I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A-I)={R}^{3}$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A-I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-I)\\ne {R}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1966 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184920} :activity/id #uuid "6d30c0b5-be79-5a7a-808d-7678444ef5c3" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc8dc1a1-9606-5b67-942e-219ab2ddfc1c"} :step/id #uuid "f2ddcf91-fcb2-581a-af17-8b331c1212e2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b51e8ff1-247b-5efd-a684-8fca6c064e1e"} :step/id #uuid "fbec4efc-b26d-5e1e-b905-eda2f46094c5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185315 :tutorial/content "A matrix is said to be **nilpotent** if some positive power of the matrix is the zero matrix. For example, the matrix\n\n$$B = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nis nilpotent because its second power is the zero matrix:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =O\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTo state the definition more precisely: a matrix with the property $B^k=O$ for a number $k>0$ is known as a **nilpotent** matrix, and the lowest number $k$ such that $B^k=O$ is called the **index.**\n\nTherefore, our matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "39a61f3b-3f47-5962-a20c-33867d6f1127" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3130 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fbfd42f1-c449-5a1c-89f3-22f0da76f2bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184918}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185316 :tutorial/content "A matrix is said to be **idempotent**, when multiplied by itself, yields itself.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$B = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nis idempotent because $B^2 = B$:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot (-6)+(-6)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ & =B\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3977ba53-51f9-53db-b487-753096fe77e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3131 :tutorial/title "Idempotent Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "bfb120ef-6495-557c-acdc-22fecb070cee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184919}}] :activity/title "Nilpotent and Idempotent Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184373} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421547} :activity/id #uuid "b53a0531-aaec-5b14-b9fe-ef1fc818e42f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265944 :question/id #uuid "73bc7e3c-0d0e-5c12-afc8-020d832fd510"} :step/id #uuid "1c5a0700-2974-52b2-a5c0-f0b093183853" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184843 :question/id #uuid "4ec9f8b4-aba4-50ea-8a5e-5c6fd75fd0f4"} :step/id #uuid "3286b5cd-eb29-594f-8a73-11ddd08bc1ef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421545 :question/id #uuid "e78f486a-816c-52d1-9895-59ae0c720ab7"} :step/id #uuid "5285a05f-cf11-5920-837d-f9098de49325"}] :activity/title "Nilpotent and Idempotent Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421533}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050492 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184159 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184063 :question/id #uuid "61a0b8a7-332d-5ae1-ad46-a65369bc87d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4415" :question/problem "Find $B^2$ given that ![](images/45/4563fac713c404455cc9787f4cd30255e79c4f4c93368f004097ee92265b6842.png)" :question/worked-solution "Computing $B^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since $B^2=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc8dc1a1-9606-5b67-942e-219ab2ddfc1c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8be0fc71-92f5-5c2d-874d-605ee8a5c69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183104} {:answer/id #uuid "97683a39-e57b-58b9-b955-92f8dc0819b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183105} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb92e79-4e53-55da-a8cc-e32142bd9ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183106} {:answer/id #uuid "4625487b-9d3f-5a2d-8cdb-df60b24c4869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183107} {:answer/id #uuid "7191d5b5-a3ec-52df-b6d5-0742678d0af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183108}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e5db1f94-1e11-5ca3-b5e3-f56dd75c4109" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183103} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60befaec-36ec-5bcc-9008-ed35714da6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390732} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e1cbf6-bcee-5647-9c18-035106ecccb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390733} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bc671c-e1db-57b1-898c-d81ac0904685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390734} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca15efe-dcb6-5128-9996-8b2ce0db2597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390735} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bcb91d-1c62-5830-889e-d2d622d846ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390734} :answer-field/id #uuid "b467dda5-da42-5b7b-b028-d10f4cde0503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390731}] :question/id #uuid "dafad545-0385-51d6-9233-37b97e1f8f85" :question/math-academy-id "q-32323" :question/problem "If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${B}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${B}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${B}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{5}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{5} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{3} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {B}^{3} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{5}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8d3b2e6-53ce-5675-9719-1e6f7e01b5ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421538} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c0b97a-bdab-58f5-a07c-79787eb3f94e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421539} {:answer/id #uuid "bec50d68-081a-5899-b3a9-04ecbfa8e6fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421540} {:answer/id #uuid "c9eefa24-dc26-5b64-9a24-78a5e468b6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421541} {:answer/id #uuid "dd800906-eb9a-5ad2-843c-4ad00d0bcb7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421538} :answer-field/id #uuid "af669ad0-ae47-5a9d-ab8c-914967692ad4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183667}] :question/id #uuid "4ec9f8b4-aba4-50ea-8a5e-5c6fd75fd0f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-78341" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${A}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${A}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $A$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${A}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c93118d-05d2-5c6b-87c7-03b3d0f94d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}45 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 45\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263663} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0d446e-c4b3-5c52-bf36-11e6accb1be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}45 & 36 \\\\ 36 & 45\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264190} {:answer/id #uuid "8b72944c-0abd-5363-81d8-0321d00b871c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264194} {:answer/id #uuid "978b8539-0071-53a5-b6bd-bfd120f218bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}72 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 72\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264303} {:answer/id #uuid "fadb6048-46a8-5f65-84e9-1d69721374d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 36 \\\\ -144 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264194} :answer-field/id #uuid "d504a61e-48ce-50a6-8766-6f3e055d4a4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265618}] :question/id #uuid "769bd8c7-2c71-5253-a7d7-b11b68ce46e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-130616" :question/problem "If $T=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${T}^{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${T}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{2} & =T\\cdot T \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${T}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $T$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${T}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{6} & ={T}^{2}\\cdot {T}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {T}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{T}^{6}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230f8658-c0ec-55d0-a480-24839035e5f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 