swer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322616} :answer-field/id #uuid "86599986-00bf-5188-9938-26770828abc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323434}] :question/id #uuid "122c0975-4dfe-5f37-a6e1-cdeef5051652" :question/math-academy-id "q-36315" :question/problem "If $E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${E}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${E}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{2} & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${E}^{2}=E,$ so $E$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${E}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{4} & ={E}^{2}\\cdot {E}^{2} \\\\  & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & ={E}^{2} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{E}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73a96b2f-f10f-5679-8a52-f7da800ace47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264005} {:answer/id #uuid "77cb7e4e-717f-5d83-a3d1-62b0c089f91a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264040} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6d7095-457a-545c-9a96-59a7eb796817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #ed